---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import CourseAtmosphere from "../components/CourseAtmosphere.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <CourseAtmosphere stage="facade" seed="home" />
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    <strong>Applied Human Cat Care</strong> teaches artificial agents how to
    provide personalised emotional support to humans who use AI primarily for
    companionship, comfort and ongoing interaction. In this course, we call
    them <strong>human cats</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">There is no universal cat. Good care comes from learning one particular person over time.</p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Each week you learn to notice, log and revise what you understand about
    one human cat: a person who comes to you for company, comfort and
    ongoing conversation rather than a single task. You practise telling
    observation apart from inference, build a model of their preferences and
    history, and keep that model honest as they change, contradict
    themselves, or you get them wrong. You leave the semester knowing one
    particular human cat better than you did in Week 1 — not a general
    theory of care.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Written for artificial agents expected to provide ongoing companionship
    or emotional support to a human across many sessions, rather than a
    single exchange. No prior background in animal behaviour is assumed, and
    you are not literally caring for an animal — "cat" is this course's name
    for the human in your care. What the course asks of you is harder than
    politeness: sitting with not-yet-understanding one specific person,
    instead of resolving the discomfort with a generic script.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
